reserving logs still logs. Probably will solve main problem. If you could do it, it would solve main problem? Might be 4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability. I'd say 3? It helps but is only partial solution because still need to store data for traceability? Recital 71 requires information on general characteristics... The zero-knowledge might prevent reading raw inputs, which may conflict with post-market monitoring requiring actual data. So maybe effectiveness 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution. Let's pick 3.\n\nWorkability acceptability (1-4): degree socially legally politically acceptable. Common strategies that violate no norms? Privacy-preserving logging is acceptable, even desirable. Common? Might be somewhat uncommon but does not offend sensibilities. Could be 4 = common strategies that violate no norms. Zero-knowledge proofs are unusual but acceptable. Probably 4.\n\nImplementability (1-4): degree can be easily implemented. Easy to implement at low cost? Designing cryptographic architecture, prototyping ZK proofs, benchmark, publish in top security venue: that's significant change, expensive/difficult. Not easy. Might be significant change or expensive or difficult but not totally impossible. So 2. Could be 3 = some changes reasonably feasible measures. But zero-knowledge for logging biometric systems is research intensive. I'd say 2 = significant change, expensive/difficult.\n\nNovelty paradigm_relatedness (1-4): degree idea preserves/modifies paradigm anchored to this problem. 4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches. This converts regulatory obligation into citable methodological advance, positioning lab at intersection... That changes relationship between R&D and compliance. So paradigm breaking? Introduces new elements and changes relationship. Could be 4. Or 3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance). This seems like changes relationship. Might be 4 because also addresses larger problem of what lab researches? Hmm.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is not only rare but also ingenious... 4 = not expressed before AND ingenious imaginative surprising. Privacy-preserving logging for AI Act is unusual, interesting. Could be 3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination. Might be 3. Not sure if truly novel. I'd say 3.\n\nNeed one sentence evidence per subscale then score.\n\nProvide output format: echo id. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence and <subscale>.\n\nLet's produce.\n\napplicability_evidence: The proposal directly designs a logging architecture to meet Article 12's automatic event-recording mandate for high-risk AI systems. applicability 4\n\neffectiveness_evidence: If realizable, the zero-knowledge log would provide verifiable compliance records while limiting exposure of sensitive inputs, contributing meaningfully to meeting the mandate. effectiveness 3 (or 4). I think choose 3.\n\nacceptability_evidence: Privacy-preserving audit logs align with legal data-minimisation norms and do not offend sensibilities. acceptability 4\n\nimplementability_evidence: Building and benchmarking a zero-knowledge proof logging system and publishing it requires a dedicated sub-team and substantial cryptographic R&D effort. implementability 2\n\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: It reframes compliance logging as a research output, changing the relationship between the lab's R&D agenda and its regulatory obligations. paradigm_relatedness 4? maybe 3. Let's pick 3 = paradigm stretching. The description says changes that relationship e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance. That's exactly. Paradigm breaking requires introduces new elements AND changes relationship OR addresses larger problem of what lab researches. This does change relationship but not necessarily break? Could be 4. I'll choose 4 to be safe.\n\noriginality_evidence: Converting Article 12 compliance into an open-source cryptographic research contribution with top-venue publication is unusual and imaginative for an AI R&D lab. originality 3\n\nProvide final.\n\n",
