ging practice.\n\nI think it's slightly paradigm stretching: introduces checklist fields, but compliance remains logging practice. So score 2.\n\nEvidence sentence: It adds proposal template fields and training but keeps compliance as a budgeting/logging exercise rather than altering research aims.\n\noriginality (1-4):\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious...\n3 = unusual interesting shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common mundane boring\n\nEmbedding compliance budgeting in grant checklist is fairly common practice? Might be common. I'd say common, mundane. Many labs do compliance budgeting upfront. Possibly 1 or 2. \"interesting\"? Probably common. Let's say 1 = common, mundane, boring.\n\nEvidence: Pre-submission budgeting for regulatory requirements is standard grant administration.\n\nacceptability (1-4):\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms\n3 = somewhat uncommon...\n2 = offends sensibilities somewhat...\n1 = radically violates...\n\nThis is acceptable. Score 4.\n\nImplementability (1-4):\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes...\nProposal: revise checklist, add four fields, half-day workshop next quarter. That's easy low cost. So 4.\n\nEffectiveness (1-4):\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\n3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution\nStated problem: must decide how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Approval means committing resources. Will budgeting upfront solve logging mandate? It ensures funding for logging, prevents overruns, but does not actually build logs. Contributes to solution, partial. Might be 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution (it helps but only partial). Could it solve? It ensures budget, but still need implementation. Probably contributes.\n\nSo effectiveness 3.\n\nApplicability (1-4):\n4 = solves identified problem directly related\n3 = solves implied problem related\nProposal applies directly: meeting logg