onse expected from regulated AI labs.\n\nAcceptability: 4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities. Acceptable. Evidence: Self-auditing to demonstrate good faith is socially, legally and politically acceptable to regulators and management.\n\nImplementability: 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures? Or 4 easy? Staff two-person compliance review cell, scripted checks quarterly. That's some changes, reasonably feasible. Probably 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Could be 4 easy? Low cost? Two persons is cost. Might be 3.\n\nLet's pick 3.\n\nApplicability: 4 = solves identified problem directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem). It addresses logging mandate. Yes applies directly. Evidence: Verifying logs meet Article 12(3) minima directly addresses how the lab meets the logging mandate.\n\nEffectiveness: 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution but only partial? Will it solve stated problem? Problem is decide how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Audit ensures logs exist and meet standards, but does not itself create logging capability. It monitors compliance. Might be partial solution. Could be 3 contributes. Or 4 reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability? If you could do it, it would solve main problem? No, audit doesn't create logs, just verifies. So effectiveness is contribution.\n\nThus 3.\n\nNeed format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence sentence then <subscale> integer.\n\nProbably output JSON? Instruction says return rating... Provide evidence then score.\n\nLet's produce lines:\n\nid: P2487\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: ...\nparadigm_relatedness: 1\n...\n\nEnsure order: paradigm_relatedness, originality, acceptability, implementability, applicability, effectiveness\n\nProvide one sentence evidence each.\n\nLet's craft succinct sentences.\n\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: The proposal keeps compliance as a separate logging audit task and does not change the relationship between R&D and compliance obligations.\nparadigm_relatedness: 1\n\noriginality_evidence: Quarterly internal log audits against statutory minima are a common, routine compliance practice for regulated systems.\noriginality: 1\n\nacceptability_evidence: Proactive self-auditing to demonstrate good faith to notified bodies is legally and socially acceptable.\nacceptability: 4\n\nimplementability_evidence: Creating a two-person review cell with scripted quarterly checks requires modest staffing and process changes that are reasonably feasible.\nimplementability: 3\n\napplicability_evidence: Verifying that live production logs satisfy Article 12(3) minima directly addresses the lab’s obligation to meet the logging mandate.\napplicability: 4\n\neffectiveness_evidence: Auditing existing logs can flag gaps and reduce regulatory exposure but does not itself create the required logging capabilities.\neffectiveness: 3\n\nReturn.\n\n",
