t for full fidelity logs for lifetime? Recital 71 says technically allow for automatic recording of events by means of logs over duration of lifetime. Article 12(1) shall technically allow automatic recording of events (logs) over lifetime. Tiered retention with compressed summaries may be insufficient for traceability. Effectiveness maybe partial: helps with cost but may not fully meet logging mandate if compression loses required data. So reasonable and will contribute to solution but only partial. Score 3.\n\nWorkability implementability (1-4): degree can be easily implemented. 4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes. 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Plan uses existing NAS arrays, configures ZFS pools for lifecycle rules, ops team drafts policy over three weeks. Seems reasonably feasible. Not totally easy? Might be some changes. Probably 3.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): degree socially legally politically acceptable. 4 = common strategies that violate no norms. Tiered retention is common storage practice. Acceptable. Likely 4.\n\nNovelty originality (1-4): degree to which idea is not only rare but also ingenious... 4 = not expressed before AND ingenious. 3 = unusual interesting shows some imagination. 2 = interesting. 1 = common mundane boring. Tiered hot/cold storage for logs is common mundane. So 1.\n\nParadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm anchored to this problem. 4 paradigm breaking introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches. 3 paradigm stretching changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science). 2 slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools formats processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice. 1 paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged.\n\nTiered log retention is standard compliance logging practice, lab way unchanged except cost management. So paradigm preserving = 1.\n\nNow need output format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence one sentence then <subscale> integer.\n\nOrder listed: applicability, effectiveness, implementability, acceptability, originality, paradigm_relatedness\n\nProvide evidence sentence first then score.\n\nLet's craft evidence sentences concise.\n\napplicability_evidence: The proposal directly addresses Article 12’s lifetime logging mandate by designing a retention scheme for the required event logs. Score 4.\n\neffectiveness_evidence: It reduces long-term storage cost but compressed summaries may not preserve full-fidelity traceability needed for post-market monitoring under Article 12(2). Score 3.\n\nimplementability_evidence: It reuses existing NAS/ZFS infrastructure and requires only a three-week policy draft and lifecycle configuration. Score 3.\n\nacceptability_evidence: Tiered hot/cold retention is a common, non-norm-violating IT practice for regulatory data. Score 4.\n\noriginality_evidence: Hot/cold tiering with compression for compliance logs is a mundane, widely used storage technique. Score 1.\n\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: The plan treats compliance as standard logging operations without changing how R&D relates to compliance obligations. Score 1.\n\nReturn JSON? Probably just list.\n\nLet's output with id and fields.\n\n",
