at violate no norms? Tying disbursements to compliance is common. Likely 4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities.\n\nImplementability: easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes. Uses existing project-management tooling, no new software procurement. Some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Could be 4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes. Adding two checkpoints into existing workflow using existing tooling sounds easy. Probably 4. Or 3? \"some changes\". Might be 4.\n\nOriginality: degree not only rare but ingenious... Not expressed before AND ingenious? This is fairly common governance practice. Probably common, mundane. Originality =1 common, mundane, boring. Could be 2 interesting? I think tying grants to compliance is not surprising. So 1.\n\nParadigm_relatedness: degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm. Anchored to this problem. Options:\n4 paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners...)\n2 slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged\n\nThis is restructuring funding gates to compliance readiness. Changes relationship between R&D and compliance obligations? It makes compliance a gating factor for funding, weaving compliance into research management. Might be paradigm stretching? It introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice? Actually it changes relationship: compliance drives funding not just science milestones. Could be 3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into the science...). Or 2 slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice.\n\nThe description: \"changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into the science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\" This seems like changing relationship. Might be 3.\n\nLet's pick paradigm_relatedness = 3? Could be 2. Hmm.\n\nThe proposal doesn't change what lab researches, just internal funding. Might be slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (checkpoints) but still treats compliance as logging practice. I think 2 is safer.\n\nCheck definitions: 4 paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what the lab researches. This does change relationship? Funding tied to compliance. Could be considered change relationship.\n\nI'll choose 3 for paradigm_relatedness? Let's decide 2.\n\nGiven typical rater, tying disbursements to compliance is a management tweak, not paradigm breaking. Slightly paradigm stretching.\n\nSo final:\neffectiveness_evidence: ... score 3\napplicability_evidence ... 4\nacceptability_evidence ...4\nimplementability_evidence...4? Maybe 3? \"some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\" Could be 3. Let's pick 4 as easy to implement at low cost using existing tooling.\n\noriginality_evidence ...1\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence...2\n\nProvide output format. Need echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>.\n\nProbably JSON.\n\nLet's output:\n\nid: P2818\neffectiveness_evidence: ...\neffectiveness: 3\n...\n\nLet's ensure one sentence evidence.\n\nProceed.",
