        "content": "{\n  \"ratings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P1644\",\n      \"paradigm_relatedness_evidence\": \"The proposal weaves logging and traceability into the scientific design gate rather than treating compliance as a post-hoc add-on.\",\n      \"paradigm_relatedness\": 3,\n      \"originality_evidence\": \"A mandatory logging-spec checkpoint at scoping is an interesting process improvement but is a familiar shift-left compliance practice in regulated R&D.\",\n      \"originality\": 2,\n      \"acceptability_evidence\": \"Adding a one-page template and a design-review check for logging is a common, norm-conforming governance practice that does not offend legal or professional sensibilities.\",\n      \"acceptability\": 4,\n      \"implementability_evidence\": \"It requires only a template addition to an existing proposal form and a review step, which is easy to implement at low cost within current project-governance.\",\n      \"implementability\": 4,\n      \"applicability_evidence\": \"Specifying logging schema, event taxonomy and retention before modelling directly addresses how the lab will meet Article 12 automatic logging and traceability mandates for new high-risk systems.\",\n      \"applicability\": 4,\n      \"effectiveness_evidence\": \"If implemented, baking traceability into architecture from day one would prevent retrofits and ensure new high-risk builds can satisfy the record-keeping and logging requirements without unplanned procurement.\",\n      \"effectiveness\": 4\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "You are a rater applying idea-evaluation instrument...\n\nWe need rating for proposal P1644.\n\nProposal: Embed Logging Specifications Into Architecture From Day One. Mandatory design-review checkpoint where every new high-risk system proposal must specify logging schema, event taxonomy, data-retention schedule before modelling work begins, making traceability first-class architectural constraint rather than post-hoc patch. Internal project-governance board adds one-page logging-spec template to existing proposal form; PIs complete during scoping, peer reviewers check feasibility against storage/compute budget before greenlighting. Baking traceability into design avoids costly mid-project retrofits and keeps new builds within existing infrastructure capacity without triggering unplanned procurement.\n\nRate subscales.\n\nparadigm_relatedness (1-4):\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way unchanged\n\nProposal makes traceability first-class architectural constraint rather than post-hoc patch. It changes relationship? Logging woven into science/design process. That's paradigm stretching? Possibly slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (design-review checkpoint, template) but still treats compliance as logging practice. Does it change relationship between R&D and compliance obligations? It makes compliance part of design gate. Might be paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (logging woven into science). The description for 3: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into the science, new partners or offerings born from compliance). That fits exactly: logging woven into science. So 3.\n\nOriginality (1-4):\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious...\n3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common, mundane, boring\n\nEmbedding logging specs early is a known best practice (shift-left compliance). Probably interesting but common? For AI labs under EU AI Act, design-review checkpoint for logging is fairly standard. Might be common, mundane. Could be interesting. I think originality = 2? Or 1? \"common, mundane, boring\" - many organizations do design review for compliance. Maybe it's interesting but not unusual. I'd say 2 = interesting. Let's pick 2.\n\nAcceptability (1-4):\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms\n3 = somewhat uncommon or unusual strategies that do not offend sensibilities\n2 = offends sensibilities somewhat...\n1 = radically violates...\n\nDesign-review checkpoint is acceptable. Common strategies. No violation. So 4.\n\nImplementability (1-4):\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change...\n1 = totally infeasible\n\nAdds one-page template to existing proposal form; PIs complete during scoping; peer reviewers check feasibility. That's low cost, non-radical. Easy to implement at low cost. So 4.\n\nApplicability (1-4):\n4 = solves an identified problem that is directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem)\n3 = solves implied problem...\n2 = may have some benefit...\n1 = intervention not stated...\n\nStated problem: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Proposal does exactly that: embed logging specs early to meet logging mandate. So 4.\n\nEffectiveness (1-4):\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem)\n3 = reasonable and will contribute...\n2 = unreasonable...\n1 = solves unrelated...\n\nWill it solve? It ensures logging schema specified before modeling, avoids retrofits, keeps within budget. That addresses compliance traceability requirement. Probably will solve main problem of meeting logging mandate for new builds. Might be partial? It doesn't address existing systems. But stated problem is decide how to respond: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. For new systems, yes solves. I'd say 4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem.\n\nCheck definitions: effectiveness rating without regard for workability. If you could do it, it would solve main problem. Seems yes.\n\nThus outputs.\n\nNeed format: echo id exactly. For each subscale in order listed: give \"<subscale>_evidence\" (one sentence) first and then \"<subscale>\" (integer).\n\nProbably output JSON? Instruction says return rating echoing its id exactly. For each subscale... give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first and then \"<subscale>\". Might be plain text.\n\nI'll produce structured lines.\n\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: ... paradigm_relatedness: 3\n\netc.\n\nLet's produce concise one sentence evidence.\n\nEnsure one sentence each.\n\nProceed.",
