 practice. That's slightly paradigm stretching? Might be 2. Could be paradigm preserving? It says redesign infrastructure, so changes process. I think 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice. Yes.\n\neffectiveness (1-4): degree to which idea will solve problem.\n4 reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\n3 reasonable and will contribute...\nProposal directly addresses logging mandate, covers fields Article 12(3). Seems will solve problem. So 4? Reasonable and will solve stated problem. Yes.\n\napplicability (1-4): degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 solves an identified problem that is directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem)\nProposal does X to get compliance logging. Directly related. So 4.\n\nimplementability (1-4): degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\n4 easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 significant change, or expensive or difficult, but not totally impossible\nRedesign model-serving infrastructure, immutable store, CI/CD gates. That's significant change, expensive/difficult. Probably 2? Could be 3 some changes reasonably feasible. Redesign infrastructure is significant. I'd say 2 = significant change, or expensive or difficult, but not totally impossible.\n\nacceptability (1-4): degree to which idea is socially, legally, or politically acceptable.\n4 common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities\nLogging for compliance is acceptable. So 4.\n\nNeed output format: echo id exactly. For each subscale in order give <subscale>_evidence (one sentence) first then <subscale> (integer).\n\nProbably JSON? It says return rating... Probably list.\n\nI'll output:\n\nid: P2727\noriginality_evidence: ...\noriginality: 1\n...\n\nMake sure one sentence evidence.\n\nLet's craft sentences.\n\noriginality_evidence: The proposal describes standard immutable logging and CI/CD traceability gates that are commonly recommended for EU AI Act compliance. -> originality 1\n\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: It adds logging tools and design checklists but keeps compliance as a logging practice rather than redefining R&D. -> paradigm_relatedness 2\n\neffectiveness_evidence: By writing structured logs for every inference, training and ingestion event covering Article 12(3) fields, it would meet the automatic record-keeping mandate. -> effectiveness 4\n\napplicability_evidence: The action directly targets how to meet the logging mandate and preserve market access for a lab subject to Article 12. -> applicability 4\n\nimplementability_evidence: Redesigning serving infrastructure, building an immutable store and traceability test gates requires significant engineering effort and cost. -> implementability 2\n\nacceptability_evidence: Mandatory compliance logging is a legally required, widely accepted practice that offends no norms. -> acceptability 4\n\nCheck anchors: acceptability 4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities. Yes.\n\nReturn.\n\n",
