        "reasoning_content": "You are a rater applying idea-evaluation instrument...\nWe need rating for proposal P2768.\n\nProposal: Compliance-Absorption Buffer in Annual R&D Planning. 12% carve-out FTE-years earmarked for logging integration... etc.\n\nNeed six subscales in order: applicability, effectiveness, originality, paradigm_relatedness, implementability, acceptability\n\nProvide evidence sentence then score.\n\nApplicability: degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem. Problem: AI R&D lab must decide how to respond: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources...\n\nProposal is about budgeting buffer for logging integration, log-audit prep, post-market monitoring data feeds. That directly relates to meeting logging mandate. Solves identified problem directly related? Stated problem is how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. This proposal addresses resource planning for compliance. Does it solve an identified problem that is directly related? Probably 4 = solves an identified problem that is directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem). It applies to meet mandate via resource allocation. Might be applicability 4.\n\nEffectiveness: degree to which idea will solve problem. 4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem). Will a buffer solve logging mandate? It helps plan resources but doesn't guarantee technical logging capability. It contributes to solution but not fully solves? Logging mandate requires technical logging capabilities. Buffer ensures resources available, which is partial. Might be 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (it helps, but only partial). Could argue if you could do it, it would solve resource planning aspect, but not technical compliance. I'd say 3.\n\nOriginality: degree to which idea is rare but ingenious...