nsibilities. Yes.\n\nNow produce output.\n\nNeed format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence sentence then <subscale> integer.\n\nProbably JSON? The prompt says return rating... give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first and then \"<subscale>\" . Might output lines.\n\nI'll produce:\n\nid: P2768\napplicability_evidence: ...\napplicability: 4\n...\n\nMake sure one sentence evidence each.\n\nLet's craft sentences.\n\nApplicability evidence: The proposal directly addresses how the lab will resource logging integration, log-audit preparation and post-market monitoring data feeds required by Article 12. Score 4.\n\nEffectiveness evidence: A ring-fenced FTE buffer would make compliance work predictable and prevent research cannibalization, but it does not itself build the technical logging capabilities mandated by Article 12. Score 3.\n\nOriginality evidence: Earmarking a fixed percentage of FTE-years as non-discretionary compliance overhead is an interesting planning tactic but not surprising or ingenious among regulated R&D organizations. Score 2? maybe 3. Hmm.\n\nLet's pick originality 2 = interesting. Might be 3? Unusual, interesting; shows some imagination. A 12% carve-out negotiated as permanent assumption might be unusual. Could be 3. Let's go with 2 to be safe? The reference class: experienced evaluator would expect labs to give responses like implement logging tools, hire compliance officer, etc. Budget buffer is plausible but not common? I'd say unusual interesting =3.\n\nParadigm_relatedness evidence: It introduces a formal budgeting element for compliance but keeps compliance as separate overhead rather than weaving logging into scientific practice or changing what the lab researches. Score 2.\n\nImplementability evidence: Securing internal council approval and renegotiating funder terms to lock in a permanent 12% overhead requires some organizational change but is reasonably feasible within annual planning cycles. Score 3.\n\nAcceptability evidence: Treating regulatory logging as a legitimate, budgeted cost of R&D is socially, legally and politically normal and does not offend norms. Score 4.\n\nReturn.\n\nLet's output nicely.",
