icability (1-4): degree clearly applies to stated problem.\nSolves identified problem directly related? The problem is how to meet logging mandate / adapt R&D. Feasibility study addresses technical viability of lifetime logging. That's directly related. So 4 = solves an identified problem that is directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem). Might be 3? Hmm. I think 4.\n\noriginality (1-4): degree not only rare but ingenious...\nFeasibility study on logging overhead is common mundane. Originality 1 = common, mundane, boring.\n\nparadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree preserves/modifies paradigm.\nParadigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged. This is just measuring overhead before design freeze. Introduces no new elements? It introduces study but still treats compliance as logging practice. So 1 = paradigm preserving.\n\nWait slightly paradigm stretching? Introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice = 2. But feasibility study is standard practice. I'd say 1.\n\nProvide evidence sentence then score.\n\nNeed format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence one sentence then <subscale> integer.\n\nProbably output JSON? Not specified. Could be list. Safer to give structured.\n\nWe'll output:\n\nid: P2698\nacceptability_evidence: ...\nacceptability: 4\netc.\n\nLet's produce.\n\n",
