        "reasoning_content": "You are a rater applying idea-evaluation instrument... Apply published anchored levels exactly, one level per subscale, and give one sentence of evidence for each rating.\n\nWe need rate proposal P1106.\n\nProposal: Annex III Biometric Logging Self-Audit Tool. Internal diagnostic tool validates whether deployed biometric identification system's logs capture all four minimum data points mandated by Article 12(3)... runs automated checks... weekly... flag logging gap before notified-body audit... fastest way to prevent enforcement action...\n\nRate subscales in order: effectiveness, applicability, implementability, acceptability, paradigm_relatedness, originality\n\neffectiveness (1-4): degree to which idea will solve problem.\nProblem: AI R&D lab must decide how to respond: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources to pursue proposal, not funding to explore whether idea might someday work.\n\nEffectiveness: Does it solve stated problem? The proposal is a self-audit tool for logging compliance specifically for Annex III biometric identification. It validates logs capture four points. That contributes to meeting logging mandate, prevents enforcement. Is it reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability? Probably will contribute but only partial solution? The mandate is broader than just Annex III biometric? The lab may have multiple high-risk systems. This addresses one product line. It helps meet logging mandate. Might be reasonable and will solve stated problem? The stated problem: must decide how to respond: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. This proposal directly addresses meeting logging mandate via monitoring. Could be considered reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability? It solves compliance for biometric logs, which is part of problem. Might be partial. Let's interpret effectiveness 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution (helps but only partial). Effectiveness 4 = will solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem). Does this solve main problem? Main problem is meeting logging mandate generally. This tool only checks one product line and only checks presence of data points, not ensure logs are technically allowed etc. Might be partial.\n\nProbably effectiveness = 3. Evidence: It ensures the mandated four log fields exist for Annex III biometric systems, directly reducing audit risk.\n\nApplicability (1-4): degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves identified problem directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem)\nThis does X: build validator. Y: ensure logs capture mandated points → part of meeting logging mandate. So applicability 4.\n\nImplementability (1-4): degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\nBuilding internal diagnostic tool that validates logs, automated weekly runs, escalate within 48h. For an AI R&D lab, building a validator is some changes, reasonably feasible. Not trivial but plausible. Probably 3. Could be 4? Might require engineering effort. I'd say 3 = some changes, reasonably feasible.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): degree to which idea is socially, legally, politically acceptable.\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities\nSelf-audit for compliance is common. Acceptable. So 4.\n\nParadigm_relatedness (1-4):\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners...)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged\n\nThis is a tool for logging compliance, still treats compliance as logging practice. Introduces new element (self-audit tool) but still compliance as logging practice. So slightly paradigm stretching? It introduces new element tools/processes but still treats compliance as logging practice => level 2.\n\nOriginality (1-4):\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious...\n3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common, mundane, boring\n\nSelf-audit tool for log compliance is common/mundane. Probably 1 or 2. Reference class: experienced evaluator would expect AI R&D labs to give responses like this. Self-audit is common. So originality 1 = common, mundane, boring.\n\nEvidence sentence each.\n\nNeed output format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence one sentence then <subscale> integer.\n\nProbably JSON? The prompt says return rating... Give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first and then \"<subscale>\". Probably list.\n\nI'll produce:\n\nid: P1106\neffectiveness_evidence: ...\neffectiveness: 3\n...\n\nMake sure each evidence is one sentence.\n\nLet's craft concise sentences.\n\neffectiveness_evidence: The tool directly checks for the four Article 12(3) log fields in live biometric systems, which would close a key compliance gap and reduce enforcement risk. That's one sentence.\n\napplicability_evidence: It implements an automated validation of mandated logging for Annex III biometric identification systems, which is a direct component of meeting the Article 12 logging mandate. Good.\n\nimplementability_evidence: Building an internal log validator and integrating weekly automated checks into post-market monitoring requires engineering effort but uses existing log data and CI pipelines. That's one sentence.\n\nacceptability_evidence: Self-auditing for regulatory compliance is a standard, legally encouraged practice that offends no norms. Good.\n\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: The proposal adds a new monitoring tool but keeps compliance as a separate logging practice rather than redefining R&D or compliance relationships. So level 2.\n\noriginality_evidence: Automated compliance self-audits for logging are a common, mundane industry response to audit mandates. Level 1.\n\nReturn.\n\nLet's ensure ordering: effectiveness, applicability, implementability, acceptability, paradigm_relatedness, originality.\n\nProvide scores.\n\nPotential nuance: effectiveness could be 4? \"reasonable and will solve the stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve the main problem)\" The stated problem is how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. For this specific product line, it would solve. But lab may have other systems. Might be partial. I'd keep 3.\n\nIf rater thinks it solves main problem for most exposed product line and protect institutional survival. Could argue 4. Hmm.\n\nThe anchored levels: 4 = reasonable and will solve the stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve the main problem). The proposal says \"fastest way to prevent enforcement action against our most exposed product line\". That's not whole lab. Might be partial.\n\nI'll go with effectiveness 3.\n\nImplementability: Could be 4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes. Automated checks on live logs maybe easy? Maybe some changes. I'll stick with 3.\n\nAcceptability 4.\n\nParadigm_relatedness 2.\n\nOriginality 1.\n\nReturn final.",
