ain problem. So 4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability.\n\nEvidence: By pre-wiring logs and docs, it ensures prototypes can be compliant immediately, addressing the core implementation burden.\n\nParadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree preserves or modifies paradigm.\nLevels: 4 paradigm breaking introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 paradigm stretching changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 slightly paradigm stretching introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 paradigm preserving standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way unchanged\n\nProposal is templates for compliance. It still treats compliance as logging practice, introduces tools/processes. That's slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice. So 2.\n\nEvidence: It adds developer tooling around logging but keeps compliance as separate plumbing to be automated rather than integrated into research.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree rare and ingenious.\nCommon? Pre-built templates for compliance is common, mundane. Probably 1 = common, mundane, boring. Or 2 = interesting? Might be somewhat unusual but not ingenious. I'd say 2 = interesting? Hmm. \"One-click compliant prototype scaffolding\" is fairly standard DevOps practice. Common. Might be 1.\n\nAnchor: 4 not expressed before AND ingenious... 3 unusual interesting shows some imagination 2 interesting 1 common mundane boring.\n\nI think this is common industry practice: scaffolding templates. So 1.\n\nEvidence: Pre-built compliance templates and CLI generators are a standard DevOps approach widely used in regulated software.\n\nReturn rating with id and subscale_evidence then subscale.\n\nNeed format? Probably JSON or lines. Provi