nd raw logs directly enables traceability, post-market monitoring and regulator evidence as required. Score 4.\n\nparadigm_relatedness (1-4):\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners...)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance...\n\nProposal is centralized repo integrated with CI/CD and quarterly reviews. That's standard compliance practice. It's slightly paradigm stretching? Introduces new elements tools, formats, processes but still treats compliance as logging practice. Likely 2.\n\nEvidence: The idea adds a tool and process for logging but keeps compliance as a separate documentation duty rather than redefining R&D. Score 2.\n\noriginality (1-4):\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious...\n3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common, mundane, boring\n\nCentralized version-controlled repo for tech docs and logs integrated with CI/CD is fairly common compliance practice. Common, mundane. Score 1.\n\nEvidence: Version-controlled documentation and log archives are commonplace industry practice for regulated software. Score 1.\n\nimplementability (1-4):\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change, or expensive...\n1 = totally infeasible\n\nNeeds platform integrated with CI/CD, dedicated compliance analyst quarterly reviews. That's some changes, reasonably feasible. Probably 3.\n\nEvidence: Deploying a document-log platform and adding analyst reviews requires moderate engineering and staffing changes but is feasible for an R&D lab. Score 3.\n\nacceptability (1-4):\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms\n3 = somewhat uncommon...\nProposal is standard compliance, acceptable. Score 4.\n\nEvidence: Maintaining technical documentation and logs to meet EU law is legally required and socially uncontroversial. Score 4.\n\nReturn rating with id and subscale_evidence then subscale.\n\nNeed format. Probably JSON? Instruction says return rating echoing its id exactly. For each subscale give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first then \"<subscale>\" integer.\n\nProbably output lines.\n\nLet's produce structured output.\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence each.\n\nLet's craft sentences concise.\n\napplicability_evidence: The proposal creates a centralized archive for technical documentation and operational logs mandated by Article 12 and Recital 71. applicability: 4\n\neffectiveness_evidence: If implemented, the versioned repository and automated stamping would provide the traceability and post-market evidence needed to meet logging requirements. effectiveness:4\n\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: It introduces a repository and CI/CD integration but treats compliance as separate logging practice without changing R&D-compliance relationship. paradigm_relatedness:2\n\noriginality_evidence: Version-controlled technical documentation and log archives with quarterly reviews are common, mundane compliance measures. originality:1\n\nimplementability_evidence: Setting up the platform and hiring a dedicated analyst involves some engineering and staffing changes but is reasonably feasible. implementability:3\n\nacceptability_evidence: Keeping logs and documentation to satisfy EU AI Act requirements is legally required and socially acceptable. acceptability:4\n\nReturn.\n\n",
