maybe uncommon. I'd say 3.\n\nParadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm anchored to this problem.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving\n\nPublishing open toolkit positions lab as sector reference point, builds market trust, gives regulators constructive reason. That changes relationship between R&D and compliance obligations? Might be paradigm stretching: new partners/offerings born from compliance. Could be 3.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is not only rare but also ingenious.\nOpen logging verification toolkit? Might be unusual, interesting; shows some imagination. Not completely novel? Could be 3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination. Or 2 = interesting. I think 3.\n\nWe need output format: echo id, then for each subscale give <subscale>_evidence sentence then <subscale> integer.\n\nProvide in order listed: applicability, effectiveness, implementability, acceptability, paradigm_relatedness, originality\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence.\n\nLet's craft sentences.\n\napplicability_evidence: The proposal creates a sector-wide verifier rather than installing logging for the lab's own high-risk systems, so it addresses an implied reputational and regulatory-engagement benefit linked to the mandate. Score 3?\n\nCheck anchor: 3 = solves an implied problem that is related to the stated problem (do X to get an implied Y, which applies to stated problem). Yes.\n\neffectiveness_evidence: A verification toolkit does not generate the Article 12 logs required for the lab's own systems and therefore will not directly meet the log