ich idea will solve problem.\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\n3 = reasonable and will contribute...\nProblem: must decide how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources.\n\nAudit program verifies logs contain minimum fields, reconstructable, survives patches. That addresses compliance but does it meet logging mandate? It ensures logs are correct, not actually create logging capability. Might be partial solution. Effectiveness? It will help ensure compliance but doesn't implement logging itself. It contributes to solution. Possibly 3.\n\nEvidence: Auditing existing logs catches gaps and enforces traceability, which contributes to meeting Article 12 but does not itself build the required logging capabilities. Score 3.\n\nApplicability (1-4): degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves an identified problem that is directly related to stated problem\n3 = solves implied problem...\nThe stated problem: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. Audit program directly addresses meeting logging mandate via verification. Seems directly related. Score 4.\n\nEvidence: The proposal directly targets traceability and Article 12(3) minimum logging fields for high-risk systems, which is the core compliance requirement. Score 4.\n\nParadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm.\nAnchors:\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners...)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged\n\nThis is internal audit program for logs. That's standard compliance practice. Introduces process but treats compliance as logging practice. Might be 1 = paradigm preserving? Or 2 slightly paradigm stretching introduces new elements tools/processes but still treats compliance as logging practice. Audit function is fairly standard. Probably paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way unchanged. Score 1.\n\nEvidence: Monthly internal sampling audits are conventional post-market surveillance practice and leave the lab's R&D workflow and its relationship to compliance unchanged. Score 1.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is rare but ingenious...\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious\n3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common, mundane, boring\n\nThis is common, mundane. Score 1.\n\nEvidence: Recurring log integrity audits are a common, mundane compliance control widely used in regulated industries. Score 1.\n\nReturn rating echoing id.\n\nFormat? Probably JSON or lines. Instruction: For each subscale give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first then \"<subscale>\". Likely output list.\n\nI'll produce:\n\nid: P2307\nacceptability_evidence: ...\nacceptability: 4\netc.\n\nProvide six pairs.\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence each.\n\nLet's craft sentences concise.\n\nAcceptability evidence: Internal log audits for Article 12 traceability are a routine, legally expected compliance measure that violates no norms. Score 4.\n\nImplementability evidence: Setting up two engineers to run monthly sampling audits requires staffing and process integration but is reasonably feasible within an R&D lab. Score 3.\n\nEffectiveness evidence: The audit programme verifies and enforces logging completeness, which helps meet Article 12 obligations but does not create the logging capability itself. Score 3? Could be 3.\n\nApplicability evidence: Verifying Article 12(3) minimum fields and reconstructability directly addresses the mandate to meet logging requireme