ng;1=common mundane boring\n\nBudget line for compliance is perhaps common? Carving fixed 8% observability line is somewhat unusual but not ingenious. Might be unusual interesting shows some imagination =3. Could be 2 interesting. Let's choose 3.\n\nParadigm_relatedness: 4 paradigm breaking introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 paradigm stretching changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners...)\n2 slightly paradigm stretching introduces new elements (tools formats processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 paradigm preserving standard compliance and logging practice; lab way unchanged\n\nThis proposal makes regulatory overhead visible cost, changes relationship? It makes compliance funded not cannibalizing. That's slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new element budget line but still treats compliance as logging practice. Not changing relationship fundamentally? Might be paradigm stretching because compliance woven into budgeting process. Hmm.\n\nThe description: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into the science, new partners or offerings born from compliance) =3\n\nIt is financial mechanism to fund logging. Is that changing relationship between R&D and compliance obligations? It makes compliance a non-negotiable cost. Might be slightly paradigm stretching =2. I'd lean 2: introduces new elements (tools formats processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice.\n\nLet's pick 2.\n\nApplicability: degree idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 solves identified problem directly related\n3 solves implied problem related\n2 may have some benefit within special situation and somehow relates\n1 intervention not stated...\n\nProblem: decide how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources to pursue proposal, not funding t