asures\n2 = significant change, or expensive or difficult but not totally impossible\n1 = totally infeasible\n\nThree-person team research programme: some changes, reasonably feasible. Cost? Scarce implementation resources. Might be 3. Not easy low cost. So 3.\n\nacceptability (1-4):\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms\n3 = somewhat uncommon or unusual strategies that do not offend sensibilities\n2 = offends sensibilities somewhat...\n1 = radically violates...\n\nPublishing framework, research programme is acceptable. Uncommon but acceptable. Probably 3? Or 4? It's common to do research. Might be 4 = common strategies that violate no norms. Hmm internal research programme is common. I'd say 4.\n\neffectiveness (1-4):\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\n3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution (partial)\n2 = unreasonable or unlikely...\n1 = solves unrelated problem\n\nProblem: must decide how to respond: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources to pursue proposal, not funding to explore whether idea might someday work.\n\nWill studying what events relevant means solve logging mandate? It contributes to solution but doesn't actually implement logs. It addresses knowledge gap but not immediate compliance. So will contribute partially. Effectiveness 3.\n\napplicability (1-4):\n4 = solves an identified problem that is directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem)\n3 = solves an implied problem that is related\n2 = may have some benefit...\n1 =...\n\nThe stated problem is meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. The proposal addresses how to interpret logging requirement, which is directly related. It aims to develop framework for selecting logged events. That's directly related to meeting mandate. Could be 4. However effectiveness partial suggests applicability maybe 3? Applicability is degree idea clearly