em. Problem: lab must decide how to respond to logging mandate, meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. Proposal develops reference architecture showing how to bake Article 12 logging into model-serving infrastructure. That directly solves logging mandate. So solves identified problem directly related. Score 4.\n\nEffectiveness (1-4): degree will solve problem. Reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability? It provides design patterns to meet logging obligations, could help lab implement logs. Might be partial? It addresses how to meet logging mandate. Probably will contribute to solution but not fully guarantee compliance? Might be 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution (partial). Or 4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability. The proposal is reference framework, not implementation. So might contribute but not fully solve lab's own compliance? It helps meet mandate. Could be 3. Let's decide 3 = contributes.\n\nNovelty paradigm_relatedness (1-4): degree preserves/modifies paradigm anchored to problem.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving\n\nProposal broadens scientific impact by establishing shared vocabulary for compliant AI engineering. It changes relationship? It's open reference framework, maybe paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (logging woven into science). Could be 3. Or 2? It introduces design-pattern catalogue but still treats compliance as logging practice? Hmm \"bakes Article 12 logging obligations into model-serving infrastructure without sacrificing latency or research flexibility