iption says paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science). That matches exactly. So 3.\n\neffectiveness: degree to which idea will solve problem. Problem: AI R&D lab subject to regulation must decide how to respond: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources to pursue proposal, not funding to explore whether idea might someday work.\n\nWill this solve problem? It meets logging mandate? It uses existing logging pipeline already being retrofitted for compliance; builds analytics layer atop. So it doesn't ensure compliance itself, but assumes logging done. Does it solve how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D? It adapts R&D to use logs. It contributes to solution? Effectiveness 4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem). Does it solve meeting logging mandate? No, it assumes logging pipeline exists. The problem includes meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. Adapting R&D is part. Might be partial solution: contributes to solution but not full compliance. Could be 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (it helps, but only partial solution). Since it doesn't ensure logs are created, just uses them. Might still be effective at adapting R&D.\n\nMaybe effectiveness 3.\n\napplicability: degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves an identified problem that is directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem)\nIdea: use logs for research. Does it directly address meeting logging mandate? It applies to adapt R&D accordingly. That's directly related. So 4? Could be 3 solves implied problem. The stated problem: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. This directly adapts R&D using logs mandated. So applicability 4.\n\nimplementability: degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\n4 = easy to implement