        "reasoning_content": "You are a rater applying idea-evaluation instrument...\n\nWe need rating for proposal P1826.\n\nProposal: Embedding Audit Logs in Experimental Design. Restructure standard experimental protocol so every high-risk system trial automatically captures full event trail required by Article 12(3)... Integrate lightweight logging layer into existing experiment-harness codebase during next two sprints, making log capture default parameter...\n\nRate six subscales.\n\nparadigm_relatedness: degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm. 4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches. 3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance). 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice. 1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working is unchanged.\n\nProposal converts compliance obligation into better experimental hygiene, logging woven into science. It changes relationship between R&D and compliance obligations. That sounds like paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (logging woven into science). Not necessarily breaking? It introduces new elements and changes relationship? Might be paradigm breaking? Breaking requires introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, OR addresses larger problem of what lab researches. This does change relationship. Does it introduce new elements? Yes lightweight logging layer. Could be 3 or 4. Paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship... or addresses larger problem of what lab researches. It changes relationship. Maybe paradigm stretching is \"changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into the science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\". That matches description exactly: logging woven into science. So 3.\n\nOriginality: 4 = not expressed before AND ingenious... 3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination. 2 = interesting. 1 = common mundane boring. Embedding logs in experimental design is probably interesting but not rare? Many labs do logging for reproducibility. Might be unusual interesting. Could be 2? Let's think: reference class is responses an experienced evaluator would expect AI R&D labs to give to this mandate. Expecting bolt-on audit module. Embedding into protocol as default is somewhat unusual but maybe common sense. Probably originality = 3? \"unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\". I'd say 2 = interesting. Hmm.\n\nThe proposal says convert compliance obligation into better experimental hygiene giving cleaner reproducibility records. That's a nice framing but not extremely ingenious. Maybe originality 3.\n\nApplicability: degree idea clearly applies to stated problem. 4 = solves an identified problem that is directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem). Problem: must decide how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. Proposal does restructure protocol to automatically capture full event trail required by Article 12(3) before analysis. That directly meets logging mandate. So applicability 4.\n\nEffectiveness: degree will solve problem. 4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability. It captures timestamps, reference database identity, matched input records – exactly Article 12(3). Making default parameter ensures automatic recording. Seems would solve main problem. So effectiveness 4? Might be partial because only addresses high-risk system trials, but problem is meet logging mandate. Reasonable and will solve. Probably 4.\n\nAcceptability: 4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities. Logging for compliance is acceptable. 4.\n\nImplementability: 4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes. Integrate lightweight logging layer into existing experiment-harness codebase during next two sprints, making log capture default parameter. That's some changes, reasonably feasible measures? Could be 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Lightweight layer in two sprints maybe easy? Might be 3 not 4. \"easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\". Integrating logging layer is some changes. Might be 3.\n\nLet's decide: implementability 3.\n\nNow produce output with id and subscale_evidence sentence then subscale integer.\n\nNeed format: For each subscale in order listed: FIRST write one sentence of evidence for that subscale (your reasoning before the number). THEN give its score as integer level.\n\nProbably output JSON? Instruction says return rating echoing its id exactly. For each subscale... give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first and then \"<subscale>\". Might be plain text.\n\nProvide lines:\n\nid: P1826\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: ...\nparadigm_relatedness: 3\n...\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence.\n\nLet's craft sentences.\n\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: The proposal weaves logging into the core experimental protocol rather than treating compliance as a separate audit add-on, changing how R&D and compliance interrelate.\nScore 3\n\noriginality_evidence: Making automatic Article 12(3) capture a default part of every training and evaluation run is unusual for labs that typically bolt on audit modules only before inspections.\nScore 3? maybe 2. Hmm.\n\nLet's pick originality 2 = interesting. \"interesting\" vs unusual interesting. The description for 3 is unusual, interesting; shows some imagination. I think it's unusual. Might be 3.\n\nLet's choose originality 3.\n\napplicability_evidence: It directly addresses the mandate to technically allow automatic recording of events and the specific Article 12(3) logging items for high-risk systems.\nScore 4\n\neffectiveness_evidence: If implemented, default capture of session timestamps, reference database identity and matched inputs would satisfy the traceability and post-market monitoring requirements without regard to cost.\nScore 4\n\nacceptability_evidence: Embedding audit logs in experimental design is a common, legally compliant strategy that does not offend scientific or regulatory sensibilities.\nScore 4\n\nimplementability_evidence: Adding a lightweight logging layer to an existing experiment-harness during two sprints requires some code changes and coordination but is reasonably feasible for an R&D lab.\nScore 3\n\nReturn.\n\n",
